    "DESCRIPTION": "FIND: `bench/immune_agents.py`, function `cytotoxic_t_cell_v2_shadow()`. The active CT v2 path accepts and emits verdicts for arbitrary `finding_id` values returned by the model, with no membership check against the requested batch and no fill-in for omitted findings. Evidence: the loop over `raw_verdicts` only does `fid = rv.get(\"finding_id\", \"\")` and `if not fid: continue`, then appends a `CellVerdict` for any non-empty ID. Unlike `cytotoxic_t_cell()`, there is no `seen` set scoped to expected finding IDs and no post-pass that emits `UNCERTAIN` for code findings the agent skipped. Because `run_immune_pipeline()` now treats CT v2 as active and feeds all its verdicts into synthesis, a malformed or hallucinated CT v2 response can inject verdicts for nonexistent findings and silently omit real findings, biasing final decisions and tool usage. This was not covered by prior findings on manifest verification or prompt structure; it is an active interface-integrity bug in the v2 verdict ingestion path.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIX:\n\n<<<<\ndef cytotoxic_t_cell_v2_shadow(\n    triaged: List[TriagedFinding],\n    source_paths: List[str],\n    timeout: int = 180,\n) -> List[CellVerdict]:\n...\n        verdicts: List[CellVerdict] = []\n        for rv in raw_verdicts:\n            fid = rv.get(\"finding_id\", \"\")\n            if not fid:\n                continue\n...\n            verdicts.append(CellVerdict(\n                cell_type=CellType.CYTOTOXIC_T,\n                finding_id=fid,\n                verdict=std_verdict,\n                confidence=round(confidence, 3),\n                evidence=(\n                    f\"[CT_v2_shadow] {v2_verdict} severity={test_severity} \"\n                    f\"{manifest_note}. {counter_evidence[:200]}\"\n                ),\n                tool_used=\"ct_v2_falsifier\",\n                elapsed_s=elapsed,\n            ))\n\n        _shadow_log.info(\n====\ndef cytotoxic_t_cell_v2_shadow(\n    triaged: List[TriagedFinding],\n    source_paths: List[str],\n    timeout: int = 180,\n) -> List[CellVerdict]:\n...\n        verdicts: List[CellVerdict] = []\n        expected_ids = {tf.finding.finding_id for tf in code_findings}\n        seen: Set[str] = set()\n        for rv in raw_verdicts:\n            fid = rv.get(\"finding_id\", \"\")\n            if not fid or fid not in expected_ids or fid in seen:\n                continue\n            seen.add(fid)\n...\n            verdicts.append(CellVerdict(\n                cell_type=CellType.CYTOTOXIC_T,\n                finding_id=fid,\n                verdict=std_verdict,\n                confidence=round(confidence, 3),\n                evidence=(\n                    f\"[CT_v2_shadow] {v2_verdict} severity={test_severity} \"\n                    f\"{manifest_note}. {counter_evidence[:200]}\"\n                ),\n                tool_used=\"ct_v2_falsifier\",\n                elapsed_s=elapsed,\n            ))\n\n        for tf in code_findings:\n            fid = tf.finding.finding_id\n            if fid not in seen:\n                verdicts.append(CellVerdict(\n                    cell_type=CellType.CYTOTOXIC_T,\n                    finding_id=fid,\n                    verdict=\"UNCERTAIN\",\n                    confidence=0.0,\n                    evidence=\"[CT_v2_shadow] Agent did not investigate this finding\",\n                    tool_used=\"ct_v2_falsifier\",\n                    elapsed_s=elapsed,\n                ))\n\n        _shadow_log.info(\n>>>>",
    "VERIFIED": true,
    "FOLLOW": "This aligns CT v2 behavior with CT v1's existing `seen`/fill-in contract and prevents `all_verdicts` in `run_immune_pipeline()` from containing off-batch verdicts. Downstream, `helper_t_v2_shadow()`, `_reconciliation_gate()`, and `ImmuneResponse.cell_verdicts` will no longer carry orphan IDs. This fix also reveals a second-order consistency issue: CT v2 currently shares `CellType.CYTOTOXIC_T` with CT v1, so helper v1 dedup-by-cell-type can collapse the two; that architectural consequence is addressed separately in R2_F072."
