    "DESCRIPTION": "FIND: `bench/verification_chain.py`, function `load_json()`. It trusts deserialized top-level `records` and `epochs` to be lists, but subsequent methods assume list semantics and indexability. Evidence: `chain._records = data.get(\"records\", [])` and `chain._epochs = data.get(\"epochs\", [])` perform no type checks. If a malformed JSON file sets `records` to a dict or string, `verify_chain()`, `head()`, `verify_record()`, and `build_inclusion_proof()` can fail with confusing runtime errors or iterate keys instead of records. This violates fail-closed expectations for a persistence verifier: malformed persisted state should be rejected at load boundary, not leak into later method behavior.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIX:\n\n<<<<\n    @classmethod\n    def load_json(cls, path: str) -> \"VerificationChain\":\n        \"\"\"Deserialize a chain from a JSON file.\n\n        Preserves the schema_version from the loaded file to prevent\n        silent version downgrades on round-trip.\n        \"\"\"\n        with open(path, \"r\") as f:\n            data = json.load(f)\n        chain = cls()\n        chain._schema_version = data.get(\"schema_version\", SCHEMA_VERSION)\n        chain._records = data.get(\"records\", [])\n        chain._epochs = data.get(\"epochs\", [])\n        return chain\n====\n    @classmethod\n    def load_json(cls, path: str) -> \"VerificationChain\":\n        \"\"\"Deserialize a chain from a JSON file.\n\n        Preserves the schema_version from the loaded file to prevent\n        silent version downgrades on round-trip.\n        \"\"\"\n        with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            data = json.load(f)\n        records = data.get(\"records\", [])\n        epochs = data.get(\"epochs\", [])\n        if not isinstance(records, list):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid chain JSON: 'records' must be a list, got {type(records).__name__}\")\n        if not isinstance(epochs, list):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid chain JSON: 'epochs' must be a list, got {type(epochs).__name__}\")\n        chain = cls()\n        chain._schema_version = data.get(\"schema_version\", SCHEMA_VERSION)\n        chain._records = records\n        chain._epochs = epochs\n        return chain\n>>>>",
    "VERIFIED": true,
    "FOLLOW": "This causes malformed files to fail immediately in CLI handlers (`record`, `verify-chain`, `verify-record`, `seal-epoch`, `prove-record`, `show-head`) instead of failing later with type-dependent exceptions. The top-level `main()` already catches `ValueError`, so the CLI path remains consistent. This does not fully validate record schema; it just closes the top-level container-type hole. More detailed schema validation still occurs lazily in `_verify_single()` and `_verify_epoch()`."
