    "description": "FIND: bench/immune_agents.py, function helper_t_cell_synthesize(), UNCERTAIN confidence calculation misreports certainty as 1.0 whenever both confirm and reject weights are nonzero but neither threshold is met. The code sets `final_confidences[fid] = max(confirm_weight, reject_weight) / max(total, 0.001)`. Because `total = confirm_weight + reject_weight`, this ratio is >= 0.5 and can approach 1.0 even when the final verdict is UNCERTAIN due to close conflict. Example from the code's own thresholds: confirm_weight=0.59, reject_weight=0.41 gives neither rejection ratio >=0.6 nor confirmation ratio >=0.4? Actually confirmation would pass there; use confirm=0.39, reject=0.61 and rejection would pass. A true UNCERTAIN case like confirm=0.35, reject=0.25 yields confidence 0.583 despite mixed conflict and sub-threshold evidence. That 'confidence' is not confidence in uncertainty; it is just the dominant side's share. This contaminates downstream reconciliation, where higher confidence wins across v1/v2 even when one side is UNCERTAIN.\n\nFIX:\n<<<<\n        else:\n            final_verdicts[fid] = \"UNCERTAIN\"\n            final_confidences[fid] = max(confirm_weight, reject_weight) / max(total, 0.001)\n====\n        else:\n            final_verdicts[fid] = \"UNCERTAIN\"\n            final_confidences[fid] = round(1.0 - (abs(confirm_weight - reject_weight) / max(total, 0.001)), 3)\n>>>>\n\nFOLLOW: This changes the meaning of v1 UNCERTAIN confidences consumed by _reconciliation_gate(). Because the gate currently lets higher confidence win regardless of verdict type, UNCERTAIN verdicts can still dominate if they carry large confidence. This fix reduces but does not eliminate that downstream issue; it reveals that _reconciliation_gate() should not compare UNCERTAIN confidence on the same footing as CONFIRMED/REJECTED. No interface changes are needed because the value remains a float in [0,1]."
