    "description": "FIND: bench/verification_chain.py, function _cli_record(). The CLI parses `args.payload` with `json.loads(args.payload)` before entering the broad main() exception handler, but it parses `args.metadata` inline inside append_record(). If metadata JSON is malformed, the chain file may already have been loaded or created, and the user gets a generic top-level error without command-specific context. More importantly, `args.metadata` is parsed separately from `payload`, so payload and metadata are not normalized/validated together even though both become part of the sealed body. This is minor for correctness but breaks the CLI's own mechanical-contract style used elsewhere.\n\nFIX:\n<<<<\n    payload = json.loads(args.payload)\n    record = chain.append_record(\n        artifact_type=args.type,\n        payload=payload,\n        recorded_by=args.by,\n        storage_mode=args.mode,\n        metadata=json.loads(args.metadata) if args.metadata else None,\n    )\n====\n    payload = json.loads(args.payload)\n    metadata = json.loads(args.metadata) if args.metadata else None\n    record = chain.append_record(\n        artifact_type=args.type,\n        payload=payload,\n        recorded_by=args.by,\n        storage_mode=args.mode,\n        metadata=metadata,\n    )\n>>>>\n\nFOLLOW: No functional interface changes. This only localizes metadata parsing, making _cli_record() easier to extend consistently with future validation around payload/metadata sealing. No downstream impact identified."
