    "DESCRIPTION": "FIND: `bench/verification_chain.py`, function `rfc9162_verify_inclusion()`. The verifier accepts arbitrary proof step hashes via `bytes.fromhex(step[\"hash\"])` without enforcing 32-byte sibling length. Evidence: any even-length hex string is accepted by `bytes.fromhex`, including empty string and 1-byte or 64-byte siblings. `_node_hash()` will hash whatever bytes are provided, so malformed proofs are processed rather than rejected structurally. This is not equivalent to the already-reported issue about ignoring proof index/root scope; it is a concrete input-validation gap in the cryptographic verifier.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIX: validate that each proof step has exactly 32 bytes and a valid side value before hashing. Exact replacement:\n<<<<\n        current = _leaf_hash(leaf_data)\n        for step in proof:\n            sibling = bytes.fromhex(step[\"hash\"])\n            if step[\"side\"] == \"right\":\n                current = _node_hash(current, sibling)\n            elif step[\"side\"] == \"left\":\n                current = _node_hash(sibling, current)\n            else:\n                return False\n        return current == expected_root\n====\n        current = _leaf_hash(leaf_data)\n        for step in proof:\n            sibling = bytes.fromhex(step[\"hash\"])\n            if len(sibling) != 32:\n                return False\n            side = step[\"side\"]\n            if side == \"right\":\n                current = _node_hash(current, sibling)\n            elif side == \"left\":\n                current = _node_hash(sibling, current)\n            else:\n                return False\n        return current == expected_root\n>>>>",
