      "DESCRIPTION": "FIND: bench/immune_agents.py, function run_immune_pipeline(), final response construction after the autoimmune override. Barrier-filtered findings are dropped from both filtered_findings and rejected_findings when observation_only=False and the skin barrier rejects them. Evidence: Stage 0 computes barrier_rejected = [f for f in new_findings if f.finding_id in failed_ids], then mutates new_findings = barrier_filtered. Later the final loop iterates only over triaged (which is built from barrier-filtered new_findings), so barrier_rejected items are absent from both filtered and rejected. The returned ImmuneResponse therefore under-reports removals and loses traceability for findings blocked before triage. This is a correctness/completeness bug in the persistence-facing API because callers cannot reconstruct which findings were removed by the active barrier.\nFIX: Include barrier-rejected findings explicitly in rejected_findings, and if autoimmune override is applied, restore them into filtered_findings as well because the documented override says 'pass everything through'. Exact replacement:\n<<<<\n    # ── Build response ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    filtered: List[Finding] = []\n    rejected: List[Finding] = []\n\n    for tf in triaged:\n        fid = tf.finding.finding_id\n        verdict = final_verdicts.get(fid, \"UNCERTAIN\")\n\n        if observation_only:\n            # Observation mode: everything passes through\n            filtered.append(tf.finding)\n        elif verdict in (\"CONFIRMED\", \"UNCERTAIN\"):\n            filtered.append(tf.finding)\n        else:\n            rejected.append(tf.finding)\n\n    # If autoimmune flagged, override: pass everything through\n    if autoimmune_flag and not observation_only:\n        filtered = [tf.finding for tf in triaged]\n        rejected = []\n\n    total = len(triaged)\n====\n    # ── Build response ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    filtered: List[Finding] = []\n    rejected: List[Finding] = list(barrier_rejected)\n\n    for tf in triaged:\n        fid = tf.finding.finding_id\n        verdict = final_verdicts.get(fid, \"UNCERTAIN\")\n\n        if observation_only:\n            # Observation mode: everything passes through\n            filtered.append(tf.finding)\n        elif verdict in (\"CONFIRMED\", \"UNCERTAIN\"):\n            filtered.append(tf.finding)\n        else:\n            rejected.append(tf.finding)\n\n    # If autoimmune flagged, override: pass everything through\n    if autoimmune_flag and not observation_only:\n        filtered = list(barrier_rejected) + [tf.finding for tf in triaged]\n        rejected = []\n\n    total = len(new_findings) + len(barrier_rejected)\n>>>>\nFOLLOW: This fix changes rejection_rate semantics. The current rej_count is derived only from final_verdicts and still excludes Stage 0 barrier removals, so after this fix the response can contain rejected_findings whose count exceeds the reported rejection_rate denominator logic. That mismatch must also be corrected. See F181.",
