      "FINDING_ID": "R6_F002",
      "SEVERITY": 0.87,
      "FLAW_CLASS": 5,
      "ABSTRACTION_INDEX": 0.74,
      "DESCRIPTION": "The verification chain records property is not actually read-only. VerificationChain.records and VerificationChain.epochs return shallow list copies whose elements are the original mutable dict objects. External callers can mutate those dicts and silently corrupt internal chain state without using append_record(), breaking append-only guarantees at the API boundary. This is a persistence-layer correctness/interface flaw, not just encapsulation style. Evidence: records returns list(self._records), not copy.deepcopy(self._records). If a caller does chain.records[0]['sealed_body']['seq'] = 999, _records[0] is mutated because the inner dict is shared.",
      "PROPOSED_FIX": "Return deep copies from records and epochs properties, matching the protection already used by head().",
      "VERIFIED": true,
      "FIND": "Location: bench/verification_chain.py, properties records and epochs. The docstrings claim 'Read-only access to the record list' and analogous for epochs, but the implementation returns only a new outer list. Since the contained elements are mutable dicts, callers can modify the chain's internal state through the returned objects. This directly contradicts the docstring and compromises integrity.",
      "FIX": "<<<<\n    @property\n    def records(self) -> list[dict]:\n        \"\"\"Read-only access to the record list.\"\"\"\n        return list(self._records)\n\n    @property\n    def epochs(self) -> list[dict]:\n        \"\"\"Read-only access to the epoch list.\"\"\"\n        return list(self._epochs)\n====\n    @property\n    def records(self) -> list[dict]:\n        \"\"\"Read-only access to the record list.\"\"\"\n        return copy.deepcopy(self._records)\n\n    @property\n    def epochs(self) -> list[dict]:\n        \"\"\"Read-only access to the epoch list.\"\"\"\n        return copy.deepcopy(self._epochs)\n>>>>",
      "FOLLOW": "This aligns records/epochs semantics with head(), which already returns copy.deepcopy(self._records[-1]). No call-site changes are required for read access. The only downstream impact is performance: copying large chains is more expensive. If that becomes material, the API should expose explicit immutable views or iterators instead of promising read-only semantics while returning shared mutable dicts."
    },
    {
      "FINDING_ID": "R6_F003",
      "SEVERITY": 0.84,
      "FLAW_CLASS": 5,
      "ABSTRACTION_INDEX": 0.71,
      "DESCRIPTION": "Verification of hash_only records is incomplete: _verify_single() never checks that payload is absent when storage_mode == 'hash_only'. A tampered or malformed record can include an unsealed extra payload field inside sealed_body while still passing verification because entry_hash is computed over the modified sealed_body itself. The contract of hash_only is that payload content is not stored in the sealed body. Evidence: _build_sealed_body() omits payload unless storage_mode == 'full_payload', but _verify_single() only conditionally verifies payload_hash for full_payload and performs no schema validation for hash_only.",
      "PROPOSED_FIX": "In _verify_single(), enforce that full_payload records include payload and hash_only records do not include payload.",
      "VERIFIED": true,
      "FIND": "Location: bench/verification_chain.py, method _verify_single(). The method verifies hashes, linkage, seq, and signatures, but does not validate storage_mode/body schema coherence. Because verification is performed against whatever sealed_body is present, not against an independently reconstructed schema, a hash_only body carrying an unexpected payload field is accepted. That breaks the storage-mode contract and can leak retained payloads where callers expect hash-only persistence.",
      "FIX": "<<<<\n        # 4. Verify payload_hash (only for full_payload mode)\n        if sealed_body[\"storage_mode\"] == \"full_payload\":\n            expected_payload = sha256_digest(\n                canonical_json(sealed_body[\"payload\"]).encode(\"utf-8\")\n            )\n            if sealed_body[\"payload_hash\"] != expected_payload:\n                return False, (\n                    f\"payload_hash mismatch: stored {sealed_body['payload_hash']}, \"\n                    f\"computed {expected_payload}\"\n                )\n\n        # 5. Verify sequence number\n====\n        # 4. Verify storage_mode/body coherence and payload_hash\n        storage_mode = sealed_body[\"storage_mode\"]\n        if storage_mode == \"full_payload\":\n            if \"payload\" not in sealed_body:\n                return False, \"full_payload record missing payload\"\n            expected_payload = sha256_digest(\n                canonical_json(sealed_body[\"payload\"]).encode(\"utf-8\")\n            )\n            if sealed_body[\"payload_hash\"] != expected_payload:\n                return False, (\n                    f\"payload_hash mismatch: stored {sealed_body['payload_hash']}, \"\n                    f\"computed {expected_payload}\"\n                )\n        elif storage_mode == \"hash_only\":\n            if \"payload\" in sealed_body:\n                return False, \"hash_only record must not contain payload\"\n        else:\n            return False, f\"invalid storage_mode in sealed body: {storage_mode!r}\"\n\n        # 5. Verify sequence number\n>>>>",
      "FOLLOW": "append_record() already enforces storage_mode membership and _build_sealed_body() already omits payload for hash_only, so this adds verification-time enforcement consistent with construction-time rules. load_json() may now surface old malformed records that previously passed; that is correct fail-closed behavior. No other functions depend on payload being present for hash_only."
    },
