      "ABSTRACTION_INDEX": 0.86,
      "DESCRIPTION": "VerificationChain.load_json() accepts arbitrary JSON and installs it directly into internal state without structural validation, while the CLI 'record' path then appends new records to that unverified chain and saves it back. This permits persistence of an already-tampered chain and continuation from an invalid head. Evidence: bench/verification_chain.py load_json() sets chain._records = data.get('records', []) and chain._epochs = data.get('epochs', []) with no call to verify_chain(). _cli_record() loads the chain, appends a new record, and saves immediately. In an append-only tamper-evident persistence layer, continuing from unverified state is a correctness defect because new valid records can be chained onto corrupted history, obscuring the corruption operationally.",
      "PROPOSED_FIX": "Fail closed on load in mutating paths: verify loaded chains before allowing append/seal/save operations, and add optional validation inside load_json().",
      "VERIFIED": true,
      "FIND": "Location: bench/verification_chain.py, methods load_json(), _cli_record(), _cli_seal_epoch(). load_json() performs deserialization only. _cli_record() does not verify the loaded chain before append_record(); _cli_seal_epoch() does verify before seal, showing inconsistent safety policy. A tampered chain file therefore remains appendable through the record CLI path.",
      "FIX": "<<<<\n    @classmethod\n    def load_json(cls, path: str) -> \"VerificationChain\":\n        \"\"\"Deserialize a chain from a JSON file.\n\n        Preserves the schema_version from the loaded file to prevent\n        silent version downgrades on round-trip.\n        \"\"\"\n        with open(path, \"r\") as f:\n            data = json.load(f)\n        chain = cls()\n        chain._schema_version = data.get(\"schema_version\", SCHEMA_VERSION)\n        chain._records = data.get(\"records\", [])\n        chain._epochs = data.get(\"epochs\", [])\n        return chain\n====\n    @classmethod\n    def load_json(\n        cls,\n        path: str,\n        *,\n        verify_on_load: bool = False,\n        verifier: Optional[\"Verifier\"] = None,\n    ) -> \"VerificationChain\":\n        \"\"\"Deserialize a chain from a JSON file.\n\n        Preserves the schema_version from the loaded file to prevent\n        silent version downgrades on round-trip.\n\n        If verify_on_load is True, fail closed if the loaded chain does not\n        verify structurally.\n        \"\"\"\n        with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            data = json.load(f)\n        chain = cls()\n        chain._schema_version = data.get(\"schema_version\", SCHEMA_VERSION)\n        chain._records = data.get(\"records\", [])\n        chain._epochs = data.get(\"epochs\", [])\n        if verify_on_load:\n            ok, msg = chain.verify_chain(verifier=verifier)\n            if not ok:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Loaded chain failed verification: {msg}\")\n        return chain\n>>>>\nAnd in CLI mutating paths:\n<<<<\n        chain = VerificationChain.load_json(chain_path)\n====\n        chain = VerificationChain.load_json(chain_path, verify_on_load=True)\n>>>>\nand\n<<<<\n    chain = VerificationChain.load_json(args.chain_file)\n====\n    chain = VerificationChain.load_json(args.chain_file, verify_on_load=True)\n>>>>",
      "FOLLOW": "verify-record, verify-chain, prove-record, and show-head may still reasonably load without eager verification because they are read/diagnostic operations; mutating operations must fail closed. This fix also means previously tolerated malformed persisted chains now raise on append/seal, which is correct for append-only integrity. If signed-chain verification on load is desired, call sites must thread a Verifier explicitly; otherwise the current structural-only verification policy remains unchanged."
    },
